<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Øresund</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oresund, the Sound, The Sound, Öresund</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Øresund, or the Sound, is the strait between the Danish island of Zealand and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, the main passage between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. At its narrowest, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a>, it is about four kilometres wide. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> levied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sound-toll">Sound Dues</a> on passing ships from 1429 until 1857, a major source of royal revenue.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oresund.jpg" alt="Øresund" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boeing720 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_%C3%98resund_new_version.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Øresund forms the Danish–Swedish border, separating Zealand from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>. It is 118 kilometres long and between 4 and 28 kilometres wide. Together with the Great Belt, the Little Belt and the Kiel Canal it is one of four waterways linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> to the Atlantic by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skagerrak">Skagerrak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. The strait is geologically young. It formed 8,500 to 8,000 years ago as rising sea levels let salt water enter the Ancylus Lake, a fresh-water body that had been connected to the sea only through the Great Belt. This marked the beginning of the modern Baltic Sea as a salt-water body.</p><p>The name is first attested on a runestone of about 1000 CE, in an Old East Norse form. Its first part is øre, a gravel or sand beach, and its second sund, a strait. The same element appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a>, Skanör and Dragør. The water is unusual: oceanic salt water lies below a depth of 10 to 15 metres, while the surface, fed from the far less salty Baltic, is much fresher in the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Political control of the strait has been an important issue in Danish and Swedish history. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> held it with the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kronborg">Kronborg</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Elsinore</a> on the western side and Kärnan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a> on the eastern side, both where the strait is 4 kilometres wide, until the eastern shore was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1658 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a>.</p><p>In 1429 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a> introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sound-toll">Sound Dues</a>, which remained in force until 1857. Tolls on waterways, roads and bridges were an accepted way of taxing and could make up a great part of a state's income. For several centuries the Dues were the most important source of income for the Danish Crown, which made Danish kings relatively independent of the privy council and the aristocracy. To escape the tolls <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> carried out two great projects: the foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a> in 1621 and the building of the Göta Canal from 1810 to 1832. The Copenhagen Convention of 1857 abolished the Dues and made the Danish straits an international waterway.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Øresund Bridge, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> and Malmö, was inaugurated on 1 July 2000 and connects a bi-national metropolitan area. The ferry route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingor">Helsingør</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a>, at the narrowest point, has long been one of the busiest international ferry routes. The strait's islands include Amager, Saltholm and the artificial Peberholm in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and Ven in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Øresund” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Denmark, including Scania east of <b>the Sound</b> until 1658, was a fertile agricultural country of grain and cattle, dominated by noble estates worked by tenant farmers; from 1733 the stavnsbånd tied Danish peasant men to the estates where they were born, until the agrarian reforms of 1788 abolished…</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch merchants, with larger ships, cheaper freight and a stronger financial base in Amsterdam, took over much of the Baltic grain and timber trade passing through the <b>Danish Sound</b>, while the herring shoals moved from the Baltic to the North Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Eric of Pomerania introduced <b>the Sound</b> Toll around 1429, a levy on ships passing Helsingør that helped fund the crown until its abolition in 1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
